name: signals
intro: A signal is a small message the kernel delivers to a process: SIGINT
intro: when you press Ctrl-C, SIGTERM from a plain `kill`, SIGCHLD to a
intro: parent whose child ended, SIGPIPE when you write to a pipe nobody
intro: reads. SIGKILL always kills and cannot be caught. Each signal has a
intro: default action: most kill the process (the wait status then says
intro: WIFSIGNALED), SIGCHLD is ignored.
intro:
intro: Change it with `sigaction`, never `signal()` -- what `signal()` does
intro: varies between systems.
intro:
intro:     static volatile sig_atomic_t hit;
intro:     static void on_int(int sig) { (void)sig; hit = 1; }
intro:
intro:     struct sigaction sa;
intro:     memset(&sa, 0, sizeof sa);
intro:     sa.sa_handler = on_int;      /* or SIG_IGN, or SIG_DFL */
intro:     sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);
intro:     if (sigaction(SIGINT, &sa, NULL) != 0) { ...fail... }
intro:
intro: A handler runs in the middle of whatever your program was doing,
intro: between any two instructions. So it may only set a flag of type
intro: `sig_atomic_t` (declared `volatile`) and call async-signal-safe
intro: functions such as `write` and `_exit` -- never `printf`, `malloc` or
intro: `exit`, which may be half-finished underneath it. Set a flag; let
intro: `main` do the real work.
intro:
intro: Standard signals do not queue: five SIGUSR1 sent while it is blocked
intro: arrive as one. `sigprocmask` blocks signals so they wait as pending.
intro: That closes a classic race: you test a flag, then sleep, and the
intro: signal lands between the two -- you sleep forever. Block the signal
intro: first, then `sigsuspend(&old)`, which unblocks and sleeps in one
intro: step.
intro:
intro: A handler that interrupts a slow call such as `read` makes it fail
intro: with `EINTR` (with `sa_flags` 0, as above; `SA_RESTART` makes it
intro: retry instead): check your flag, or retry.
intro:
intro: `kill(pid, SIGTERM)` sends a signal to another process. Build a set
intro: with `sigemptyset(&s); sigaddset(&s, SIGUSR1);` and block it with
intro: `sigprocmask(SIG_BLOCK, &s, &old)`. `sigpending(&p)` plus
intro: `sigismember(&p, SIGUSR1)` ask what is waiting.
intro:
intro: SIGPIPE kills a writer silently. A tool that wants to report the
intro: error itself sets it to SIG_IGN, and `write` then fails with
intro: `EPIPE`.
intro:
intro: Never order things with `sleep`. To know a child is ready, have it
intro: write a byte down a pipe, and wait for that byte.
---
q: sigcount.c -- catch a signal N times
spec: Take two arguments: a signal NAME, one of `INT`, `TERM`, `HUP`,
spec: `USR1`, `USR2`, `CHLD` (meaning SIGINT and so on), and a count N from
spec: 0 to 100. Install a handler for that signal with `sigaction`, send
spec: the signal to yourself N times with `raise`, then print
spec: `caught C`, where C is how many times the handler ran. Exit 0.
spec:
spec: The handler may only touch a `volatile sig_atomic_t` counter. For
spec: an unblocked signal `raise` does not return until the handler has
spec: run, so C comes out equal to N.
spec:
spec: Any other arguments (wrong count, unknown NAME, N not a whole
spec: number in range) print `usage: sigcount NAME N` to standard error
spec: and exit 2. If `sigaction` fails print `sigcount: sigaction failed`
spec: to standard error and exit 1.
file: sigcount.c
prog: #define _POSIX_C_SOURCE 200809L
prog: #include <errno.h>
prog: #include <signal.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: #include <stdlib.h>
prog: #include <string.h>
prog: 
prog: static volatile sig_atomic_t count;
prog: 
prog: static void on_signal(int sig)
prog: {
prog: 	(void)sig;
prog: 	count = count + 1;
prog: }
prog: 
prog: static const struct {
prog: 	const char *name;
prog: 	int sig;
prog: } names[] = {
prog: 	{"INT", SIGINT}, {"TERM", SIGTERM}, {"HUP", SIGHUP},
prog: 	{"USR1", SIGUSR1}, {"USR2", SIGUSR2}, {"CHLD", SIGCHLD},
prog: };
prog: 
prog: int main(int argc, char **argv)
prog: {
prog: 	int sig = 0;
prog: 	long n = -1;
prog: 	if (argc == 3) {
prog: 		for (size_t i = 0; i < sizeof names / sizeof names[0]; i++)
prog: 			if (strcmp(argv[1], names[i].name) == 0)
prog: 				sig = names[i].sig;
prog: 		char *end;
prog: 		errno = 0;
prog: 		n = strtol(argv[2], &end, 10);
prog: 		if (errno != 0 || end == argv[2] || *end != '\0')
prog: 			n = -1;
prog: 	}
prog: 	if (sig == 0 || n < 0 || n > 100) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "usage: sigcount NAME N\n");
prog: 		return 2;
prog: 	}
prog: 	struct sigaction sa;
prog: 	memset(&sa, 0, sizeof sa);
prog: 	sa.sa_handler = on_signal;
prog: 	sigemptyset(&sa.sa_mask);
prog: 	if (sigaction(sig, &sa, NULL) != 0) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "sigcount: sigaction failed\n");
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 	for (long i = 0; i < n; i++)
prog: 		raise(sig);
prog: 	printf("caught %d\n", (int)count);
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: sigcount USR1 3
case: sigcount INT 1; sigcount TERM 0
case: sigcount CHLD 5
case: sigcount HUP 100
case: sigcount USR2 101; echo $?
case: sigcount KILL 1; echo $?
case: sigcount USR1; echo $?
hint: a static volatile sig_atomic_t counter, a handler that adds one, sigaction to install it for the chosen signal, then raise(sig) in a loop
